thank you for being uh with us mr  mcdonald so  we just heard from kierstarma who said  that he would like to see all  schools open to all pupils from the 8th  of march now of course that's not what  the teaching unions are saying  who do you think is right in this debate  well the teaching unions are saying very  similar to kier that  of course we want the schools to open  but it's got to be safe  and i can't think of anyone better to  listen to  than those on the front line and dara is  the teachers unions it's interesting  i don't think this has happened very  often but all the teachers union  including those who  represent the head teachers and school  leaders as well as the  frontline teaching staff themselves all  saying the same thing  so i think listen to the unions and i  don't think that's different from what  kiara is saying kira is saying is when  it's safe and  if we have to have a staggered reopening  of the schools  let's listen to those on the front line  okay you're also calling today  for a lot of the debts built up during  the pandemic to be  cancelled so how much debt are we  talking about and how would you pay for  it  well at the moment what we're finding is  well look at the figures that have come  out this morning we've now got nearly  half a million people  not just living in poverty but in  destitution  and if you look at what's coming from  the citizens advice bureau and the  the debt campaigning organization step  change we have now  about a million well just over a million  in severe debt  someone said the one of the calculations  from step change was  there's about a 10 billion pound debt  that's been caused by coving  on individuals and families themselves  it's a fair amount of money  but even if we just made a start on that  let's take this six hundred thousand who  are behind with their rents and  their mortgages we're talking about a  billion pounds worth that's needed  to take that pressure off them and the  way that you do that  is well you know we lifted the debt off  the banks when the banks were in crisis  hundreds of billions were lifted off  them by taking the debt over by the bank  of england and therefore  service that much more cheaply but  actually if you did want to pay for it  in one fell swoop  why not have a windfall tax on those  companies in the well the banks and the  property developers  tech companies and others maybe some of  those  cronies have had contracts during the  covid crisis as well  why not a windfall tax on them to lift  the debt off the poorest and you know  one in five of those in serious debt are  key workers the very people are getting  us through this pandemic  so i think it's one of those policies  that's readily available in fact  you know boris johnson and others have  referred back to the period after the  second world war  rad butler as a conservative chancellor  in 51  actually introduced a windfall tax on  those companies that made vast profits  during the war and post-war period so  it's something that's been done  time and time again i also note that  you've said today  there's a time for framing speeches and  political positioning  but there is also a time to get angry  and demand amnesia action to help  people survive who exactly is that  message meant for  for all of us for all of us here today  framing speech during the week where he  for me you know basically saying we need  to be as radical as the  atlea government and we need to tackle  inequality as our main  central thrust of policy that suits me  fine  what we need to do now is roll that out  and i think all of us both  towards the labor front base labour and  peace all those progressives actually  commentators as well i think need to get  serious with holding this government to  account  and actually i am i am angry people are  suffering you know  people those in debt but also those who  are struggling to get to work have had  their wages cut  and i am angry my constituents have been  treated badly during this pandemic only  two miles down the road from here i have  heathrow limited a company that is now  doing fire and higher sacking people and  then only re-employing them when they  cut their wages  outlawing fire and rehire as a policy i  think is something  we should all be arguing for now and yes  i am angry about why  people are living in poverty you know a  lot of those people on the front line  who are trying to get us through this  pandemic some of them are having to live  off food banks that's unacceptable  and yet this government is now growing  flicked upon many of them  another pay freeze so yeah there's a lot  to be angry about and i think we need to  hold this government to account i'm glad  i'm glad you asked hancock about the con  contracts i wish you had gone further  and asked him is it a requirement to do  donate to the tory party to get one of  these contracts because that's what it  appears to be at the moment  because this vip route for these  contracts so yes i think people should  get angry  and should start demanding  accountability from this government  and demanding change do you think that  kier stamos should get a bit angry then  i think we're now moving into the phase  where yes  i don't think it's just on kei's  shoulders i think it's for all of us all  of us who care about our community  whether you're a politician or a member  of the community or yes journalists as  well it's time to hold this government  to account  120 000 dead you know food banks now  having to support so many people  homelessness rising again  we've got a situation where we're now  for a budget we've only just heard the  furlough be extended that decision  should have been made months ago the  government looks as though it's going to  hold on to  the 20 pound increase in universal  credit yet living off universal credit  is thrusting people into  well destitution as we've seen and the  figures today  we need action from this government we  need to hold it to account and yes we  need to be putting forward alternatives  now  we can't wait until the next election we  need to be bringing forward the policies  that can  provide people with hope and a vision of  a better society  kir's speech during the week framed this  debate  now we've got to drive it forward um  do you think that people who voted for  kirsten in the leadership election  thinking that he would continue on some  of the radical policies under yourself  and jeremy corbyn  may have been a bit disappointed  well look i took the view and jeremy did  the same is that when we stood down  jeremy's leader sherry charles we would  not  treat the incoming leadership in the way  that we were treated  carping from the sidelines et cetera  what we said we'd do is that  we would support them all we could and  we have done that  and now what we want to do is support  them in the rollout of  a radical policy program the ways in  which we hold the  well hold this government to account in  the way in which we can present an  alternative  to this government but what i'm saying  is you can't leave that until so close  to the next election  if if we've learned anything you need to  present that vision but then  start presenting the details that will  enable us to achieve that vision  and that does mean starting early rather  than leaving it too late because you've  got to  win the argument can i also say just one  word of advice as  which you and jeremy corbyn failed to do  winning an election  well the way if you want to learn  anything from us  we had an early election thrust upon us  in many  ways we lost the opportunity of bedding  down and winning the argument  over the vision of the society we want  to create all the individual policies  you need to start early  and that's what i'm saying let's get on  with that now let's really now  hold this government to account let's  really now set out  that clear vision exactly as kate did  during the week that  radicalness of the athlete government  tackling inequality but let's start  talking about the alternative program  the detail of those policies i have to  say also my only  real word of advice to kyra is well  let's unite the party as he promised and  that does mean  you know giving the whip back to jeremy  corbyn let's unite the party so we can  move on  and really be an effective opposition  holding this government to account but  presenting an alternative  and also the way we are at the moment  doing everything we can to support those  people  who are suffering so much under this  government okay  uh john mcdonald thank you very much for  being on the programme this morning  thank you

